UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4: Tell a story your listener can follow', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A clear oral retell uses brief cue notes for connected events, introduces who and where, and rehearses pace so a listener can follow without a full script.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nShorten \"Then she carefully followed the colourful signs past many tall shelves\" into four cue words and say where you would pause.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCues: follow—signs—past—shelves. Pause after the problem (moved club) before the solution.\n\nCHECK\nReading a full script aloud is not the same as telling from cues; listeners need connected events, not every adjective.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPrepare four cue lines for an original four-event story, tell it in four to six spoken sentences, note one listener question or self-check, and record one rehearsal improvement.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"A clear oral retell uses brief cue notes for connected events, introduces who and where, and rehearses pace so a listener can follow without a full script.","lesson_number":4,"lesson_role":"Speaking and handwriting","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-OLC-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Prepare four cue lines for an original four-event story, tell it in four to six spoken sentences, note one listener question or self-check, and record one rehearsal improvement. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using retell on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Reading a full script aloud is not the same as telling from cues;. Correction: Cues: follow—signs—past—shelves.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Reading a full script aloud is not the same as telling from cues; listeners need connected events, not every adjective. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating retell on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with retell, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about retell with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for retell. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for retell. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Reading a full script aloud is not the same as telling from cues","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Cues: follow—signs—past—shelves"],"question":"Shorten \"Then she carefully followed the colourful signs past many tall shelves\" into four cue words and say where you would pause. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for retell; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Cues: follow—signs—past—shelves","Careful use of cue notes and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Reading a full script aloud is not the same as telling from cues","A clear oral retell uses brief cue notes for connected events, introduces who and where, and rehearses pace so a"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with retell?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named cue notes and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Reading a full script aloud is not the same as telling from cues","Name cue notes and explain why it matters only, without linking it to retell","Apply retell to new material and name the cue notes and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies retell with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries retell. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event","Say the writing is good without naming any words","Reading a full script aloud is not the same as telling from cues","Point to exact words in the sentence that show retell and say what they do"],"question":"Read: \"In the dusty shed, Lila oiled the bike chain before the race.\" Which choice best uses retell from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Name cue notes and explain why it matters without linking it to any words in the text","Cues: follow—signs—past—shelves","Reading a full script aloud is not the same as telling from cues"],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about retell?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Guess from the title alone with no textual clue","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea","Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding"],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse cue notes and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess"],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for retell?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about retell","Retype the worked model as the final answer","Change fonts and claim the work is new","Reading a full script aloud is not the same as telling from cues"],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of retell on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","mar