ound the invitation. Next she raced to the library…\" Pause after the problem; check the listener can name the goal.\n\nTRY FIRST\nShorten \"Then she carefully followed the colourful signs past many tall shelves\" into four cue words and say where you would pause.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCues: follow—signs—past—shelves. Pause after the problem (moved club) before the solution.","explain":"A clear oral retell uses brief cue notes for connected events, introduces who and where, and rehearses pace so a listener can follow without a full script.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Reading a full script aloud is not the same as telling from cues; listeners need connected events, not every adjective. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Cue notes supporting an oral retell.","caption":"Pause after the problem for listeners.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Cues</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">kitchen; library; notice; gate</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Spoken</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">complete sentences from cues</text></g></svg>"}],